WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.750
Begin with our January
Monday request at eight,

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:07.910
supported by two hundred and forty
eight training hours for its key.

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:13.070
Change to Monday, June fourth,
still at eight and still a working day.

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:17.190
The forecast remains three hundred
and forty nine point five zero.

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:19.610
Both requests are valid.

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:22.850
The selected predictor
simply has no month term,

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:25.010
so they share the same stored parameter.

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:30.460
An unchanged prediction does not establish
that January and June should have equal

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:31.170
demand.

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:34.430
It tells us what this fitted
function can distinguish,

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:38.230
which is a separate question from
whether the input is well formed.

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:45.696
A valid Sunday request at eight selects
the nonworking pair and returns seventy

00:00:45.696 --> 00:00:50.550
six point four three, supported by one
hundred and fifteen training observations.

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:52.790
The date, weekday

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:55.790
and working day flag change
together consistently.

00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:00.990
The Monday quiet hour uses another
key and returns seven point five one.

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:06.300
These comparisons expose the actual lookup
behavior without fitting or reading an

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:06.830
outcome.

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:11.320
They are not causal estimates of changing
one flag while holding an impossible

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:12.490
calendar fixed.

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:17.980
We retain four accepted cases with their
support and fallback status so the model

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:20.070
response can be traced concretely.

00:01:22.208 --> 00:01:26.858
A large miss found afterward makes
the pooling assumption tangible.

00:01:27.748 --> 00:01:30.098
On Monday, April sixteenth at eight,

00:01:30.308 --> 00:01:33.838
the source records a holiday
and a nonworking flag.

00:01:34.368 --> 00:01:39.278
The model therefore uses the nonworking
mean of seventy six point four three,

00:01:39.588 --> 00:01:42.318
while the observed count is
five hundred and ninety seven.

00:01:43.328 --> 00:01:48.798
A holiday can be structurally consistent
without behaving like a typical Sunday.

00:01:49.548 --> 00:01:53.858
We do not change the recorded flag
afterward to obtain a better answer.

00:01:54.348 --> 00:01:59.718
This was a posthoc error example,
not a predeclared extra request probe.

00:02:00.388 --> 00:02:03.018
The validator checks
structure and consistency,

00:02:03.508 --> 00:02:06.698
while calendar authority must
come from the trusted source.

00:02:08.858 --> 00:02:13.648
The validator accepts exactly the
schema version, target timestamp,

00:02:14.198 --> 00:02:15.108
issue timestamp

00:02:15.538 --> 00:02:17.168
and named calendar inputs.

00:02:17.918 --> 00:02:23.258
It checks exact hourly naive timestamps
and requires the issue to be one hour

00:02:23.258 --> 00:02:24.808
before the target interval.

00:02:25.418 --> 00:02:31.068
It also checks that encoded hour, month
and weekday agree with that timestamp.

00:02:32.058 --> 00:02:36.888
Named dictionary order is harmless because
encoding follows the declared schema.

00:02:37.738 --> 00:02:40.768
Reordering the saved model
feature names is rejected.

00:02:41.618 --> 00:02:45.228
Equal vector lengths cannot
identify misplaced feature meanings.

00:02:46.298 --> 00:02:50.608
The fourteen rejected probes include
missing and nonfinite fields,

00:02:51.078 --> 00:02:52.808
unknown categories and schema,

00:02:53.278 --> 00:02:54.508
extra outcomes,

00:02:54.938 --> 00:02:56.148
inconsistent flags,

00:02:56.518 --> 00:02:59.708
wrong lead and unsupported
timestamp forms.

00:03:01.858 --> 00:03:06.858
The candidate currently supports
historical validation replay from January

00:03:06.858 --> 00:03:10.508
first at one in the morning through
June thirtieth at twenty three.

00:03:11.358 --> 00:03:14.948
The lower boundary follows the
frozen one hour lead convention.

00:03:15.958 --> 00:03:20.008
Reserved test dates and a future
twenty thirteen request are rejected

00:03:20.538 --> 00:03:22.608
even if their calendar
values could be encoded.

00:03:23.398 --> 00:03:27.888
The dataset leaves timezone and daylight
saving interpretation unspecified,

00:03:28.498 --> 00:03:32.708
so this review preserves naive timestamps
rather than inventing an offset.

00:03:33.398 --> 00:03:36.788
This narrow domain records
the evidence we actually have.

00:03:37.278 --> 00:03:41.008
A later application needs its own
explicit serving and date policy;

00:03:41.618 --> 00:03:44.768
that adapter is not implemented
by these sensitivity probes.

00:03:47.018 --> 00:03:49.228
Three cases need different behavior.

00:03:49.978 --> 00:03:53.948
A valid hour with no source
observation may receive an estimate,

00:03:54.198 --> 00:03:56.968
but no zero target is created for scoring.

00:03:57.678 --> 00:04:03.238
A valid hour and working day pair absent
from training uses the saved training only

00:04:03.238 --> 00:04:06.688
global mean, one hundred and
forty three point seven nine.

00:04:07.078 --> 00:04:11.688
All forty eight real pairs are present,
so a small fixture checks this branch.

00:04:12.398 --> 00:04:17.388
A missing field or unknown schema
instead causes rejection before lookup.

00:04:17.838 --> 00:04:21.008
Fallback is not permission
to repair malformed input.

00:04:21.458 --> 00:04:25.098
Keeping those distinctions prevents
coverage gaps and unsupported requests

00:04:25.098 --> 00:04:28.868
from being hidden behind an
apparently successful numeric answer.

